"""DSA foundations with executable hand-offs, authored independently of fixtures."""
from .content import lesson, track

DSA = track('dsa-foundations', 'dsa', 'DSA concepts and invariants',
            'Fourteen focused concept lessons; executable practice remains a separate evidence source.', (
    lesson('dsa-complexity', 'Time, space and growth', (),
           ('Count work as input grows.', 'Separate auxiliary storage from input storage.'),
           'Asymptotic complexity describes growth, not wall-clock seconds. A loop visiting every element once performs n visits; constant work per visit gives linear time. Auxiliary space counts additional memory. Two consecutive linear scans remain linear; nested full scans multiply their work.',
           'For [4, 1, 7, 2], a maximum scan compares each value with the best seen so far: 4, 4, 7, 7. Doubling the list roughly doubles comparisons and needs only one best-value variable.',
           ('Count visits rather than numeric magnitudes.', 'There is one visit per input element.', 'n visits times constant work grows linearly.'),
           'A single full scan over n values does constant work per value. Its tight time bound is?',
           ('O(1)', 'O(log n)', 'O(n)', 'O(n^2)'), 'O(n)',
           'There are n constant-cost visits, so the tight growth is linear; no nested scan multiplies it.'),
    lesson('dsa-arrays', 'Arrays and index boundaries', ('dsa-complexity',),
           ('Use half-open index ranges.', 'Distinguish an index from a stored value.'),
           'An array gives indexed access but the final valid index is length minus one. A half-open range [start, end) includes start and excludes end, making its length end-start. Empty ranges are valid. Traversals should define which positions are inspected before accessing neighbors.',
           'In [8, 3, 9, 2, 6], the range [1, 4) visits indices 1, 2 and 3 and yields [3, 9, 2]. Index 4 belongs to the next range, and [2, 2) contains no positions.',
           ('The right endpoint is excluded.', 'List indices starting at start and stopping before end.', 'Subtract start from end to count positions.'),
           'How many elements belong to the half-open index range [2, 7)?',
           ('4', '5', '6', '7'), '5', 'The included indices are 2, 3, 4, 5 and 6: 7-2 equals five.'),
    lesson('dsa-prefix', 'Prefix sums and range subtraction', ('dsa-arrays',),
           ('Construct a prefix array with an initial zero.', 'Compute range sums without rescanning.'),
           'Define prefix[0]=0 and prefix[i+1]=prefix[i]+a[i]. Then the sum over [l, r) equals prefix[r]-prefix[l]. The leading zero handles ranges beginning at zero without a special case. Preprocessing costs linear time and storage; each later sum query costs constant time.',
           'For [3, -2, 5, 1], prefix is [0, 3, 1, 6, 7]. The range [1, 3) includes -2 and 5, giving prefix[3]-prefix[1]=6-3=3, not a sum that includes index 3.',
           ('Use the exclusive right boundary.', 'Subtract the total before the left boundary.', 'prefix[3] is 6 and prefix[1] is 3.'),
           'For [3,-2,5,1], what is the sum over [1,3)?', ('2', '3', '4', '6'), '3',
           'Only -2 and 5 are included; their sum is 3, matching the prefix difference.'),
    lesson('dsa-window', 'Sliding windows and monotonicity', ('dsa-prefix',),
           ('Identify the positivity assumption.', 'Trace expand and shrink operations.'),
           'For positive numbers, expanding a window cannot decrease its sum and shrinking cannot increase it. This monotonicity permits two pointers for the shortest window meeting a target. Negative numbers break that reasoning: removing a negative can increase the sum, so a positive-only method must not silently accept them.',
           'For [2, 3, 1, 2] and target 5, expanding the first two values reaches 5 with length 2. Removing 2 drops to 3. Later [3,1,2] reaches 6 but cannot beat the earlier length 2.',
           ('Consider what adding one value does to a sum.', 'A negative value can decrease the sum on expansion.', 'The advertised positive-only specification supplies monotonicity.'),
           'Which assumption justifies this expand/shrink sum monotonicity?',
           ('All values are positive', 'The array is sorted', 'Values are distinct', 'The target is even'),
           'All values are positive', 'Positive additions increase the sum and removals decrease it; sorting or distinctness is not sufficient.',
           target='/practice/ex1_shortest_subarray?spec=v1_positive_only'),
    lesson('dsa-binary-search', 'Binary search as a boundary invariant', ('dsa-arrays',),
           ('Find the first value at least a target.', 'Maintain the excluded and candidate regions.'),
           'Lower-bound search partitions a sorted array into values below the target and values at least the target. Keep a half-open candidate interval [lo, hi). If a[mid] is too small, set lo=mid+1; otherwise retain mid by setting hi=mid. Equality must move the right boundary to find the first duplicate.',
           'For [1, 4, 4, 9] and target 4, lo=0, hi=4. mid=2 contains 4, so hi=2. mid=1 also contains 4, so hi=1. mid=0 contains 1, so lo=1. The first qualifying index is 1.',
           ('Equality is not a reason to stop at an arbitrary duplicate.', 'Keep the midpoint when it already qualifies.', 'Move hi to mid on equality.'),
           'In [1,4,4,9], the zero-based first index with value at least 4 is?',
           ('0', '1', '2', '3'), '1', 'Index 1 is the first 4; index 2 qualifies but is not the first boundary.',
           target='/practice/ex2_binary_search?spec=v1_sorted_with_duplicates'),
    lesson('dsa-hashing', 'Hash membership and distinct positions', ('dsa-arrays',),
           ('Track only previously visited values.', 'Avoid reusing one position in a pair.'),
           'A hash set supports expected constant-time membership under ordinary hash distribution, not a universal worst-case guarantee. For pair sums, check target-current against previously seen values before inserting current. This ordering ensures the two positions are distinct even when equal values form the pair.',
           'For [3, 3] and target 6, the first 3 finds no earlier complement and is inserted. The second 3 finds the earlier 3 and forms a valid pair. For the one-element list [3], no pair exists.',
           ('Ask whether the complement came from an earlier position.', 'Insertion before checking can match the current value to itself.', 'Check membership first, then insert.'),
           'Does the one-element list [3] contain two distinct positions summing to 6?',
           ('Yes', 'No', 'Only if sorted', 'Only with a hash map'), 'No',
           'A value cannot supply both positions; the single occurrence has no earlier complement.'),
    lesson('dsa-stack', 'Stacks and nested structure', ('dsa-arrays',),
           ('Apply last-in-first-out order.', 'Detect mismatched and unmatched delimiters.'),
           'A stack models obligations that must be discharged in reverse arrival order. On an opening delimiter, push its expected closing delimiter. On a closing delimiter, compare it with the top before popping. Reject an empty-stack close, a mismatch, or a nonempty stack at the end.',
           'For ([{}]), push ), then ], then }. Reading } discharges the newest obligation, followed by ] and ). For ([)], the next close ) conflicts with the expected ], so the sequence is invalid.',
           ('Track the most recent unmatched opening delimiter.', 'The top after ([ is ].', 'A ) cannot discharge an expected ].'),
           'Which delimiter string is correctly nested?', ('([)]', '([])', '(()', ']['), '([])',
           'The square pair closes before its containing round pair, and no unmatched obligations remain.'),
    lesson('dsa-linked-list', 'Linked lists and safe rewiring', ('dsa-arrays',),
           ('Trace node references rather than array indices.', 'Preserve the unvisited suffix while reversing.'),
           'A singly linked list stores a next reference in each node; indexed access requires traversal. Reversal maintains a reversed prefix and an unvisited suffix. Save current.next before changing it, link current to the previous node, then advance using the saved reference. Losing that saved suffix loses the rest of the traversal.',
           'For A->B->C, save B before setting A.next=null. Then save C before setting B.next=A. Finally set C.next=B. The new head is C and the old head A becomes the tail.',
           ('A next reference is overwritten during reversal.', 'Save the old next node before the overwrite.', 'Advance current to the saved successor.'),
           'What must be saved before overwriting current.next during reversal?',
           ('The old successor', 'The list length', 'The tail value', 'The array index'), 'The old successor',
           'The old successor retains access to the unvisited suffix; otherwise rewiring can make it unreachable.'),
    lesson('dsa-tree', 'Binary trees and search ordering', ('dsa-linked-list',),
           ('Distinguish a binary tree from a search tree.', 'Use inorder traversal only with a defined ordering policy.'),
           'A binary tree has at most two children per node; a binary search tree adds an ordering invariant. For distinct keys, every left-subtree key is smaller and every right-subtree key larger. Inorder traversal visits left, node, right, producing sorted keys only when that search invariant holds.',
           'A search tree with root 5, left child 2 and right child 8 visits 2,5,8 inorder. Putting 9 under the left child violates the ancestor bound even if 9 is greater than its immediate parent 2.',
           ('Inorder visits left subtree before the node.', 'The node comes before its right subtree.', 'The example visits 2, then 5, then 8.'),
           'The inorder sequence for root 5 with children 2 and 8 is?',
           ('5,2,8', '2,5,8', '8,5,2', '2,8,5'), '2,5,8',
           'Inorder is left-node-right, and this tree satisfies the distinct-key search ordering.'),
    lesson('dsa-heap', 'Heaps and priority queues', ('dsa-tree',),
           ('Use a min-heap for repeated minimum removal.', 'Avoid assuming the heap is globally sorted.'),
           'A min-heap guarantees each parent is no larger than its children; it does not sort siblings or whole levels. The root supplies the current minimum. Insertion and minimum removal restore the local invariant along one tree path, taking logarithmic time in a binary heap.',
           'Insert priorities 7,2,5 into a min-heap. Its root is 2; removing that root returns 2 and restores a heap containing 5 and 7. The next removal returns 5, regardless of the original insertion order.',
           ('The minimum lives at the root.', 'Removal restores the heap invariant.', 'Among 7,2,5 the first two minima are 2 and 5.'),
           'Which two priorities are removed first from a min-heap containing 7,2,5?',
           ('7,2', '2,5', '5,2', '7,5'), '2,5',
           'A min-heap removes the smallest remaining priority each time, giving 2 followed by 5.'),
    lesson('dsa-graph', 'Graph traversal and shortest hops', ('dsa-stack', 'dsa-tree'),
           ('Use breadth-first layers in an unweighted graph.', 'Mark discovery to avoid repeated queueing.'),
           'Breadth-first search uses a FIFO queue to explore distance layers in an unweighted graph. Mark vertices when enqueued, not only when removed, to avoid duplicate discovery. BFS yields minimum edge counts, not minimum weighted cost; weighted edges require a different shortest-path argument.',
           'With edges A-B, A-C, B-D and C-D, BFS discovers B and C at distance 1 and D at distance 2. Marking D on first discovery prevents the other branch from enqueueing it again.',
           ('Count edges, not vertex labels.', 'D is one edge beyond B or C.', 'A->B->D contains two edges.'),
           'In that graph, what is the shortest number of edges from A to D?',
           ('1', '2', '3', '4'), '2', 'Either A-B-D or A-C-D uses two edges; no direct A-D edge exists.', difficulty='intermediate'),
    lesson('dsa-dp', 'Dynamic programming state sufficiency', ('dsa-prefix',),
           ('Define what each state summarizes.', 'Retain both extremes when sign changes matter.'),
           'Dynamic programming reuses results for overlapping subproblems, but the state must preserve all information needed by future transitions. In maximum-product subarrays, a negative multiplier can turn the smallest product into the largest. Track maximum and minimum products ending at each position, and permit a fresh start.',
           'For [2,-3,-4], start max=min=2. At -3, candidate products are -3 and -6, so max=-3 and min=-6. At -4, candidates -4,12,24 make the new maximum 24. Keeping only the old maximum would miss it.',
           ('Multiplication by a negative reverses order.', 'The most negative earlier product may become largest.', 'Keep maximum and minimum ending products.'),
           'Which state is necessary for the standard linear maximum-product transition?',
           ('Only the running sum', 'Only the largest product', 'Both maximum and minimum ending products', 'Only the input length'),
           'Both maximum and minimum ending products', 'A negative multiplier swaps the roles of extremes; the old minimum can generate the new maximum.',
           difficulty='intermediate', target='/practice/ex3_max_product?spec=v1_bounded_ints'),
    lesson('dsa-greedy', 'Greedy choices and exchange arguments', ('dsa-complexity',),
           ('Justify a local choice with an exchange argument.', 'Distinguish interval count from interval weight.'),
           'A greedy algorithm needs a proof that its local choice preserves an optimal solution. For maximizing the count of nonoverlapping half-open intervals, choose the earliest finishing compatible interval. Replacing an optimal first interval by one ending earlier leaves at least as much room. Weighted rewards invalidate this count-based proof.',
           'Intervals [0,2), [1,5), [2,3), [3,4) permit the earliest-finish choices [0,2), [2,3), [3,4), totaling three. Picking the longest interval [1,5) instead leaves fewer compatible choices.',
           ('Optimize count, not duration or reward.', 'An earlier finish leaves more room.', 'Select the compatible interval with smallest end time.'),
           'For unweighted interval scheduling, which compatible interval is chosen by the proven greedy rule?',
           ('Earliest finishing', 'Longest duration', 'Latest starting', 'Highest label'), 'Earliest finishing',
           'The exchange argument replaces the first choice by an earlier finish without reducing available future space.', difficulty='intermediate'),
    lesson('dsa-sort', 'Stable sorting and retained order', ('dsa-arrays',),
           ('Define stability for equal keys.', 'Separate ordering from deduplication.'),
           'A stable sort preserves input order among elements with equal sort keys. Sorting does not normally remove duplicate records. This matters when a previous order is meaningful, such as timestamp order among equal priorities. An unstable sort may still be correctly ordered by key while losing that secondary order.',
           'Records (A,2),(B,1),(C,2) sorted stably by the number become (B,1),(A,2),(C,2). A stays before C because both have key 2. Removing C would be deduplication, a different operation.',
           ('Compare the equal-key records A and C.', 'Their input order should survive.', 'B moves first, then A remains before C.'),
           'Which order is the stable ascending-key result for (A,2),(B,1),(C,2)?',
           ('B,A,C', 'B,C,A', 'A,B,C', 'C,A,B'), 'B,A,C',
           'B has the smaller key, and equal-key A and C retain their original relative order.'),
))
